WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.080 --> 00:00:03.870
- Being diagnosed
definitely saved my life,

00:00:03.870 --> 00:00:05.340
and I'm very open about that

00:00:05.340 --> 00:00:06.780
because I think it's not something

00:00:06.780 --> 00:00:08.403
that's talked about enough.

00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:11.910
Autism, first of all,
is very underdiagnosed,

00:00:11.910 --> 00:00:14.610
especially in women because
it presents differently.

00:00:14.610 --> 00:00:17.340
Growing up, I always felt so different,

00:00:17.340 --> 00:00:19.860
and I felt like I didn't belong.

00:00:19.860 --> 00:00:22.410
I didn't understand why I was interested

00:00:22.410 --> 00:00:23.340
in different things,

00:00:23.340 --> 00:00:26.190
or when it came to social communications,

00:00:26.190 --> 00:00:27.720
like, there was this disconnect.

00:00:27.720 --> 00:00:29.670
I didn't understand why that was.

00:00:29.670 --> 00:00:33.870
I felt very isolated and separated,

00:00:33.870 --> 00:00:36.960
and I wasn't happy with who I was,

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:38.810
and I felt like I had to change that.

00:00:39.864 --> 00:00:42.447
(bright music)

00:00:43.890 --> 00:00:45.870
My name is Olivia Schlaepfer.

00:00:45.870 --> 00:00:47.880
I'm a senior here at Yale College,

00:00:47.880 --> 00:00:50.133
and I'm in the
Environmental Studies major.

00:00:51.570 --> 00:00:55.773
I am neurodiverse, I am
autistic, and I also have ADHD.

00:01:00.150 --> 00:01:03.180
Since being diagnosed and
since being here at Yale,

00:01:03.180 --> 00:01:05.190
I've really come into my own.

00:01:05.190 --> 00:01:08.280
I accept who I am and I'm
also a lot easier on myself

00:01:08.280 --> 00:01:11.310
because I know my brain
is structured differently

00:01:11.310 --> 00:01:13.590
and that comes with so
many amazing things.

00:01:13.590 --> 00:01:16.800
Like, I delight in such
a very rich experience

00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:19.170
in this world from a sensory perspective.

00:01:19.170 --> 00:01:21.720
I feel very connected
to our natural world.

00:01:21.720 --> 00:01:24.273
That's why I'm in an
Environmental Studies major,

00:01:25.650 --> 00:01:27.993
but it does also lead
to some difficulties.

00:01:31.800 --> 00:01:34.770
Some of the things that I
struggled academically with,

00:01:34.770 --> 00:01:38.610
in my first year before
getting diagnosed, for example,

00:01:38.610 --> 00:01:40.710
in long lectures, during class,

00:01:40.710 --> 00:01:43.530
I'd have a lot of trouble sitting still,

00:01:43.530 --> 00:01:45.180
especially lecture formats.

00:01:45.180 --> 00:01:49.260
I couldn't stay focused
for that amount of time.

00:01:49.260 --> 00:01:51.630
If professors were just writing
things on the chalkboard

00:01:51.630 --> 00:01:53.700
and there weren't slides
for me to consult,

00:01:53.700 --> 00:01:55.320
I felt very lost.

00:01:55.320 --> 00:01:57.840
I had a lot of trouble
being able to stay focused

00:01:57.840 --> 00:01:58.920
and take notes.

00:01:58.920 --> 00:02:00.900
Even though I wanted to learn,

00:02:00.900 --> 00:02:03.690
there was some block where I couldn't

00:02:03.690 --> 00:02:05.790
and that led to a lot of anxiety

00:02:05.790 --> 00:02:08.910
because I felt, like, I
didn't know what was wrong.

00:02:08.910 --> 00:02:11.460
I didn't know why I wasn't
able to do the things

00:02:11.460 --> 00:02:13.500
that I'd been able to do in the past,

00:02:13.500 --> 00:02:16.050
or I didn't know why other
people weren't struggling

00:02:16.050 --> 00:02:16.890
with these things.

00:02:16.890 --> 00:02:20.820
I'd go into exams and it
would take me a lot longer

00:02:20.820 --> 00:02:23.040
to understand the questions,

00:02:23.040 --> 00:02:25.980
and sometimes I'd be so
sure that I understood it

00:02:25.980 --> 00:02:28.050
and yet I would interpret it differently

00:02:28.050 --> 00:02:29.640
and I would misinterpret it,

00:02:29.640 --> 00:02:32.850
and I wouldn't understand why that was.

00:02:32.850 --> 00:02:35.070
And I also wasn't able to finish exams

00:02:35.070 --> 00:02:36.930
because it would take me longer

00:02:36.930 --> 00:02:38.460
to be able to sort through the material

00:02:38.460 --> 00:02:39.990
or to understand the question.

00:02:39.990 --> 00:02:41.790
I would have to be, you know, asking

00:02:41.790 --> 00:02:42.990
like raising my hand

00:02:42.990 --> 00:02:45.990
and I would feel very
awkward and uncomfortable

00:02:45.990 --> 00:02:48.000
having to clarify things

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:50.100
and then maybe the professor
wouldn't understand

00:02:50.100 --> 00:02:53.684
why I didn't understand and
I also didn't understand,

00:02:53.684 --> 00:02:54.750
and there was a lot of uncertainty

00:02:54.750 --> 00:02:57.060
and that did create a lot of anxiety

00:02:57.060 --> 00:02:59.520
and being able to get this diagnosis

00:02:59.520 --> 00:03:03.030
to educate myself on, okay,
this makes so much sense.

00:03:03.030 --> 00:03:06.120
I have all of these answers
now to why I feel this way

00:03:06.120 --> 00:03:09.480
and why I experience the
world the way that I do.

00:03:09.480 --> 00:03:12.810
And it's so much knowledge that I have now

00:03:12.810 --> 00:03:16.050
and that's really allowed
me to accept myself.

00:03:16.050 --> 00:03:19.020
And that's why now I'm
so open about things

00:03:19.020 --> 00:03:20.610
is because I want to help other people

00:03:20.610 --> 00:03:22.950
and be a voice for
those who are struggling

00:03:22.950 --> 00:03:24.660
and don't know why

00:03:24.660 --> 00:03:28.470
and haven't reached out for
help yet or in the process

00:03:28.470 --> 00:03:32.100
and aren't feeling like
they love themselves

00:03:32.100 --> 00:03:33.240
for who they are.

00:03:33.240 --> 00:03:36.510
And I want to show that
it's okay to be different

00:03:36.510 --> 00:03:39.300
and being neurodiverse is beautiful,

00:03:39.300 --> 00:03:42.060
and it allows us to bring
a different perspective

00:03:42.060 --> 00:03:43.402
to this world.

00:03:43.402 --> 00:03:45.985
(upbeat music)

00:03:48.600 --> 00:03:51.630
For students coming into
Yale who are autistic

00:03:51.630 --> 00:03:54.540
or have ADHD or another disability,

00:03:54.540 --> 00:03:57.570
I know that it can be
stressful and overwhelming

00:03:57.570 --> 00:03:58.920
and you don't know where to start.

00:03:58.920 --> 00:04:00.000
It's a new environment.

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:01.620
You're coming into college,

00:04:01.620 --> 00:04:05.550
and it really does take you
out of your comfort zone.

00:04:05.550 --> 00:04:09.367
It can be intimidating to
approach professors and be like,

00:04:09.367 --> 00:04:12.600
"Actually, I do experience
the world in a different way.

00:04:12.600 --> 00:04:14.070
I do learn differently."

00:04:14.070 --> 00:04:15.720
But I would say my experience

00:04:15.720 --> 00:04:18.780
with professors has been
overwhelmingly positive.

00:04:18.780 --> 00:04:20.790
I would say just at the
start of the semester

00:04:20.790 --> 00:04:23.370
to open that door for
communication and to be honest.

00:04:23.370 --> 00:04:25.200
Professors are people, too.

00:04:25.200 --> 00:04:28.740
They're people who have
their own world experiences

00:04:28.740 --> 00:04:31.950
and can probably relate
to a lot of the struggles

00:04:31.950 --> 00:04:33.960
that you go with, you deal with,

00:04:33.960 --> 00:04:37.262
even if it's not
precisely in the same way.

00:04:37.262 --> 00:04:39.845
(bright music)

00:04:41.010 --> 00:04:44.580
You have so many different
resources to help you here,

00:04:44.580 --> 00:04:46.860
whether that's your first-year
counselor, your dean,

00:04:46.860 --> 00:04:50.550
your head of college, also
Mental Health &amp; Counseling,

00:04:50.550 --> 00:04:54.180
Student Accessibility
Services, Poorvu Center.

00:04:54.180 --> 00:04:56.070
There's so many different avenues

00:04:56.070 --> 00:04:58.740
for you to get the help
and the support you need.

00:04:58.740 --> 00:05:01.800
And I think there is still a
lot of stigma about, you know,

00:05:01.800 --> 00:05:02.640
getting support,

00:05:02.640 --> 00:05:05.880
getting whether that
be tutoring or therapy

00:05:05.880 --> 00:05:08.010
or academic accommodations

00:05:08.010 --> 00:05:10.410
because it can make you feel less than,

00:05:10.410 --> 00:05:12.570
like, "Oh, I'm not good enough."

00:05:12.570 --> 00:05:14.700
But that's not how you should see it.

00:05:14.700 --> 00:05:17.010
It should be, it's not that
you're not good enough,

00:05:17.010 --> 00:05:18.960
it's that you learn in a different way.

00:05:18.960 --> 00:05:23.100
Our academic system is set
up for a certain neurotype,

00:05:23.100 --> 00:05:24.990
for people who learn a certain way,

00:05:24.990 --> 00:05:26.640
and academic accommodations

00:05:26.640 --> 00:05:28.710
that helps to level the playing field

00:05:28.710 --> 00:05:30.570
and to ensure fairness.

00:05:30.570 --> 00:05:34.110
It's not giving a leg up
or it's not oh, extra help.

00:05:34.110 --> 00:05:36.420
No, it's allowing for there to be fairness

00:05:36.420 --> 00:05:38.490
and ensuring there's an even playing field

00:05:38.490 --> 00:05:41.373
so that everyone here at Yale can thrive.

